WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:09.950
In 1934, a French book did the maths on insect flight. Using airplane rules, the answer was: impossible.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.490
Bees didn't read the book.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:19.050
The mistake? A bee's wing isn't an airplane wing. It doesn't glide. It sweeps.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:27.210
A honeybee beats its wings about two hundred and thirty times a second, back and forth, over a short arc.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.620
At the end of every stroke, the wing flips over.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:36.150
Each sweep spins up a tiny whirlwind along the wing's front edge.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:43.630
That swirl is low pressure. It pulls the wing up: extra lift that airplane maths never counted.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:49.210
And in thinner air, bees don't flap faster. They swing their wings wider.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:54.150
Bees could always fly. We just needed better maths.
